#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064006 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7bd68b3-56ae-57e5-81ef-2370c96664c4" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/id #uuid "15473603-5bb5-5a20-8fc9-e4d7589ccb38" :question/math-academy-id "e-473" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}\\left(\\cot x + \\sin^2 x\\right)\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x } = \\csc x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064007 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/id #uuid "68d2eb66-87ab-5182-9669-376bd1f70673" :question/math-academy-id "e-474" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x =  \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\dfrac{\\sin x }{\\cos x}  = \\tan x$ and $\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} = \\sec x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\sec x\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a standard trigonometric integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064008 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d309fd9-62bd-5307-9dfc-128f9ff028aa" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "b34db891-0676-505c-9ba3-57d86f1d9b94" :question/math-academy-id "e-475" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\tan 2x = \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{\\cos 2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNotice that the numerator of the integrand is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we can solve this by substitution.\n\nLet $u=\\cos 2x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=-2 \\sin 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\textrm d u= \\sin 2x \\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{-1}|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebe05d70-e334-50fa-82ab-657e120476de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\sqrt{2}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300148} {:answer/id #uuid "d098fb97-95f7-5f86-a8aa-a83703db2038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300149} {:answer/id #uuid "39cc65ca-fb96-563f-811a-c0e00f5e0a7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300150} {:answer/id #uuid "86cf893e-69da-553f-a4e9-4fb26df5615c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300151} {:answer/id #uuid "187fd51c-418f-5182-a575-d02a9b2d1076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\sqrt{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39cc65ca-fb96-563f-811a-c0e00f5e0a7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300150} :answer-field/id #uuid "6954e32c-c94f-5b26-902c-8cb55225375f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90fb5794-ccc5-5bbb-84a7-0497aefc34e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-114306" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =3[\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡0] \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90c6c6df-c0db-5186-b953-2b0fb9f31ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300155} {:answer/id #uuid "c99da369-f5ac-523b-b0cc-5a3fb4bb74c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300156} {:answer/id #uuid "8dbeadf8-1a95-5a05-926c-031574f13c95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300157} {:answer/id #uuid "d47c6437-a1fb-5960-9720-e18bbfd3d39c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300158} {:answer/id #uuid "5bfe218a-4298-5d1a-8d4a-21bc096f7b0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d47c6437-a1fb-5960-9720-e18bbfd3d39c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300158} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ccdb9d8-a843-5e82-93e0-6e7eb65611de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8642bcc0-89fd-586c-8c7c-00d4c39709e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-114313" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d309fd9-62bd-5307-9dfc-128f9ff028aa" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1730c4a-1cab-567c-bc2c-f5931f316bd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1730c4a-1cab-567c-bc2c-f5931f316bd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300162} :answer-field/id #uuid "527507f2-7464-5fa6-a11e-d163834e2854" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fc1564b1-ddad-50e0-ae4c-ee2d033f736e" :question/math-academy-id "q-352786" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{3}\\,x\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{3}\\,x\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}\\,x\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}-{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(8-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d309fd9-62bd-5307-9dfc-128f9ff028aa" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "276d12cf-e996-5357-b64d-096f04206208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300165} {:answer/id #uuid "1d67476d-5b1f-5fb2-8008-3de35a910411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "276d12cf-e996-5357-b64d-096f04206208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300165} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bfea8b5-982e-51c7-94d0-286a2e511620" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "75c4ab91-ebb5-5fe9-9351-221cd3ae76d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-352797" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this by substitution. Let $u=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{1}^{3/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b9a0236-0378-5579-93c8-a43981adbfb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b9a0236-0378-5579-93c8-a43981adbfb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300169} :answer-field/id #uuid "22869a33-682a-53cb-977f-c168b27b8d28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c38935ed-4bc6-5d43-868b-d331452b0131" :question/math-academy-id "q-352853" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the identities $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$  and  $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})-(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff66d701-ccee-551c-96f2-ca7da2e776d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298554} {:answer/id #uuid "fe47818b-fb2f-50d3-98b2-39d200c448c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff66d701-ccee-551c-96f2-ca7da2e776d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298554} :answer-field/id #uuid "12018ba6-d93d-59f7-9f7e-13760e380cde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5555438-060c-5bd3-a19d-6d830dc51bce" :question/math-academy-id "q-352868" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=4x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7bd68b3-56ae-57e5-81ef-2370c96664c4" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "844e4f20-3499-56a7-b3ba-041ac2f7e222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec x - 2\\cos x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063084} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a923ef-ba2c-556e-ba9b-9749fd371054" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan x - 2\\sin x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063085} {:answer/id #uuid "dc566e4e-6547-53f2-a82d-90720318a4bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\tan x + 2\\sin x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063086} {:answer/id #uuid "f25bc6ba-d12b-559f-9425-46b15ce536a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec x + 2\\cos x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "844e4f20-3499-56a7-b3ba-041ac2f7e222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec x - 2\\cos x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063084} :answer-field/id #uuid "452eae41-5b36-5f3b-b709-8daa01c4bad2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8beed7b3-f15a-500c-aa6e-e151d8a3ea87" :question/math-academy-id "q-49070" :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7bd68b3-56ae-57e5-81ef-2370c96664c4" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9da9a697-2470-5eab-ac75-025dd2c42471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300173} {:answer/id #uuid "4ece5e31-a3b6-557c-86ca-d097b4e71653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300174} {:answer/id #uuid "1eeaf580-58e6-5ce7-ab34-109a63893baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300175} {:answer/id #uuid "6a22cc0d-3ce6-5c0b-87df-ddcbb555b548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300176} {:answer/id #uuid "12ac93e4-48da-5e7d-8e86-449073272e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9da9a697-2470-5eab-ac75-025dd2c42471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300173} :answer-field/id #uuid "a278f1cb-b741-5a0d-ad86-3d531580b8ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4267ace3-7026-57ec-b5b0-b39e5d715bde" :question/math-academy-id "q-49258" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d309fd9-62bd-5307-9dfc-128f9ff028aa" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a18e1647-4d67-547c-817b-4bf88fe7a925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (\\ln \\mid x \\mid) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063143} {:answer/id #uuid "a9bcfa03-a7b5-5f75-831a-32b948aa1346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid \\sin x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063144} {:answer/id #uuid "c61d5908-5c61-5619-be9c-4c5dbbea3949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid \\csc x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063145} {:answer/id #uuid "dc21e438-0b01-592e-80b8-66a108ab490b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063146} {:answer/id #uuid "691ba9e1-89d0-515a-bea7-548f429be701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\csc (\\ln \\mid x \\mid) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9bcfa03-a7b5-5f75-831a-32b948aa1346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid \\sin x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063144} :answer-field/id #uuid "05533db7-22be-587e-a29e-1772ab898e00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eeffd6d0-ba71-5af0-8a18-c001ad19ba1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-49259" :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7bd68b3-56ae-57e5-81ef-2370c96664c4" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0131869-e77c-53ea-9a97-8a419d11e0be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300180} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff04e81-bf94-5a5e-968a-6569b54db209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300181} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2a3299-9f90-5596-838d-cff5c0c4952a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300182} {:answer/id #uuid "1f2a87d6-846e-5eed-a0d5-7cc2064f58d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300183} {:answer/id #uuid "e1323c3f-2c75-50d9-a04e-46433f2de703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec2a3299-9f90-5596-838d-cff5c0c4952a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300182} :answer-field/id #uuid "7838c7bc-0311-52aa-aac9-395894888917" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9bc58fe3-4afd-5b0d-a72b-13bb8464c51a" :question/math-academy-id "q-49272" :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identities $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7bd68b3-56ae-57e5-81ef-2370c96664c4" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e934fdd4-48f8-57b6-8b36-6345994e6b25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300187} {:answer/id #uuid "d505a148-400f-53ad-a1c7-c377d5f5be58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300188} {:answer/id #uuid "dd796922-c3b9-5375-825d-c29714a164bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300189} {:answer/id #uuid "271791bc-439e-5a0d-b7ee-3a9d5e2ba501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300190} {:answer/id #uuid "0891d2cb-a28a-5b5c-991c-79e9c10b04f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0891d2cb-a28a-5b5c-991c-79e9c10b04f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300191} :answer-field/id #uuid "db25d327-4092-5212-b343-acab5c528514" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c407cd3a-68bd-5db6-8cf4-b08f6bf2ac1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-49274" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d309fd9-62bd-5307-9dfc-128f9ff028aa" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ed4b054-3977-51b1-a7f4-c1aeefeb871e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300194} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf5336e-f675-5794-b628-7e93fc0b9143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300195} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba0753d-3ab7-5a40-bc0b-f5f07a498521" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300196} {:answer/id #uuid "02011bf2-46cb-516c-8c78-6db3fd69d5e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300197} {:answer/id #uuid "65fc1d7d-598c-57e5-b3c7-bcef672dfd7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cf5336e-f675-5794-b628-7e93fc0b9143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300195} :answer-field/id #uuid "01b62ac7-9351-542b-a6b3-7727956f560b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a20189d-02fe-55f5-871e-83e3f87395e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-49278" :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)^{-1}|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d309fd9-62bd-5307-9dfc-128f9ff028aa" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0374ace-3185-5f45-9639-79ce4494cf5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{tan}^{2}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300201} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ee91c7-d405-56f1-b102-1875a9f26cb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300202} {:answer/id #uuid "089934f9-52e8-50d7-9a4d-304d048364ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300203} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c44999-74d7-5c74-b945-63ecb2d8d3ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300204} {:answer/id #uuid "9080a115-8bce-5471-a440-9d603cc94588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5ee91c7-d405-56f1-b102-1875a9f26cb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300202} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7708ddb-84df-5021-9323-7836c0f0e9c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07bc96d4-2114-5d28-917f-2b7ffe5f207c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49280" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the identity $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7bd68b3-56ae-57e5-81ef-2370c96664c4" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c93ba90b-3f50-5023-9616-4888404c88cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300208} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2915cc-2ec4-5ce7-91bf-7bd31941fbed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300209} {:answer/id #uuid "3ffd38aa-dc3b-5624-8442-f55807557453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300210} {:answer/id #uuid "345ed6a6-e213-50c0-bc26-3d82566c387e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300211} {:answer/id #uuid "55b650bb-4e4a-5aae-a0ac-bbcdbdfe81de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ffd38aa-dc3b-5624-8442-f55807557453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300210} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b4a1100-5b8a-5db0-9078-e78b1a8b368f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "379e9498-17d7-5258-a37f-d94516f74aac" :question/math-academy-id "q-51109" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=2x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡u}{2}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a87016a-964b-54a6-8d1d-8b7347307c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300215} {:answer/id #uuid "e88dd7d7-1743-59f0-bb84-1bde1ca3a50c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300216} {:answer/id #uuid "3db8725f-db52-5ce1-b009-c06d371ac1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300217} {:answer/id #uuid "97a7e750-0c3b-59ef-b2e8-5a581387defb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300218} {:answer/id #uuid "8e20ad80-43ad-5fad-a90a-286dc5619cc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97a7e750-0c3b-59ef-b2e8-5a581387defb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300218} :answer-field/id #uuid "639f496d-dbd9-5f8e-9575-94aa252fbdd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee6a19b5-6f42-5348-add1-abb3ba7985cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-73361" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
